---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Glance from "../../components/Glance.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="scorecard-gamification"
  team="PM, EM & engineering (incl. architect)"
  readTime="7 min read"
  coverPlain
  coverWidth="max-w-[70%]"
  coverShadow
  externalCategory="External"
  externalLabel="Blog post"
  externalHref="https://newrelic.com/blog/observability/how-to-drive-engineering-excellence-through-gamified-scorecards"
>
  <Glance challengeLabel="Challenge" strategyLabel="Solution" impactLabel="Outcome">
    <Fragment slot="challenge">
      Scorecards usage was low, and that meant more than a UX gap: it meant{" "}
      <strong>lost revenue</strong>. Compliance managers, meanwhile, had no way
      to flag which rules mattered most.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="strategy">
      I led the direction and aligned Engineering and Product behind it. The
      result: a gamified layer fit to New Relic's own rule-based model, with{" "}
      <strong>maturity levels, weights, and a team leaderboard</strong>.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="impact">
      <strong>+12% Scorecards usage, +8% lift in advanced Compute Consumption
      Units (aCCU)</strong>, the metric the suite is priced on, after launch.
      Teams can now see how they level up, what to fix next, and how they
      rank.
    </Fragment>
  </Glance>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 sm:px-10">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Scorecards is how New Relic customers hold their engineering organization
        to a bar: quality, security, ownership, production readiness. It measures
        services against rules the customer defines and rolls the results into a
        score. But a score on a dashboard doesn't, by itself, change how teams
        behave. Our data analysis showed usage was low, and customer conversations
        told us why: because every rule counted equally, compliance managers
        couldn't flag what carried the most risk, and engineering teams had no way
        to tell what to fix first.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>

  <CaseSection title="Who we designed for">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The work served two roles: <strong>engineering leaders</strong> (primary)
        and <strong>compliance managers</strong> (secondary). Each pain point maps
        to a specific part of the solution:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Engineering leaders struggle with low standards adoption across
          teams.</strong> Levels and a team leaderboard turn improvement into
          visible progress and healthy competition.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compliance managers can't express what matters most.</strong>{" "}
          Configurable weights let them prioritize the rules that carry the most
          risk, so a score reflects real priorities rather than counting every rule
          equally.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Leaders lack a read on where to invest.</strong> Maturity
          distribution and trends over time pinpoint the teams and services that
          need support to reach the next level.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection title="Approach">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Rather than rebuild Scorecards to match how competitors do gamification,
        we layered it onto the model New Relic already had. That meant three
        connected moves —
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maturity levels</strong> to give teams something to climb,</li>
        <li><strong>Weights</strong> to give rules different priorities, and</li>
        <li><strong>A leaderboard</strong> to turn progress into healthy competition.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The hard part was fitting each onto a rule-based model that was never
        designed for leveling. The sections below walk through how.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="01" title="How Scorecards actually work">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Every design decision came back to one thing: how a scorecard actually
        gets scored. It works like this:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A Scorecard is a set of rules.</li>
        <li>
          Each rule is a query that checks a group of entities (services,
          browser apps, and so on) and marks each one pass or fail.
        </li>
        <li>
          A rule's score is entities passed ÷ entities checked, and the
          scorecard's score is the average of its rules.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>

    <figure class="my-10">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 px-4 py-6 sm:px-6">
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 760 300"
          class="mx-auto w-full max-w-2xl"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Concept diagram: entities roll up into a rule score, and rules average into a scorecard score"
          fill="none"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif"
        >
          <defs>
            <marker id="scArrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="#6c685f" />
            </marker>
          </defs>

          <!-- Column labels -->
          <text x="20" y="20" fill="#6c685f" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">ENTITIES</text>
          <text x="235" y="20" fill="#6c685f" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">RULES</text>
          <text x="565" y="20" fill="#6c685f" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">SCORECARD</text>

          <!-- Entities: row 1 (2/5) -->
          <circle cx="24" cy="75" r="6" fill="#0041d9"/>
          <circle cx="44" cy="75" r="6" fill="#0041d9"/>
          <circle cx="64" cy="75" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <circle cx="84" cy="75" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <circle cx="104" cy="75" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <!-- Entities: row 2 (3/5) -->
          <circle cx="24" cy="150" r="6" fill="#0041d9"/>
          <circle cx="44" cy="150" r="6" fill="#0041d9"/>
          <circle cx="64" cy="150" r="6" fill="#0041d9"/>
          <circle cx="84" cy="150" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <circle cx="104" cy="150" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <!-- Entities: row 3 (1/5) -->
          <circle cx="24" cy="225" r="6" fill="#0041d9"/>
          <circle cx="44" cy="225" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <circle cx="64" cy="225" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <circle cx="84" cy="225" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
          <circle cx="104" cy="225" r="6" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>

          <!-- Entities -> Rules -->
          <line x1="122" y1="75" x2="205" y2="75" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#scArrow)"/>
          <line x1="122" y1="150" x2="205" y2="150" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#scArrow)"/>
          <line x1="122" y1="225" x2="205" y2="225" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#scArrow)"/>

          <!-- Rule nodes -->
          <rect x="212" y="53" width="168" height="44" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="228" y="72" fill="#514d45" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Rule</text>
          <text x="228" y="88" fill="#6c685f" font-size="10" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">a query</text>
          <text x="364" y="80" fill="#514d45" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">40%</text>

          <rect x="212" y="128" width="168" height="44" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="228" y="147" fill="#514d45" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Rule</text>
          <text x="228" y="163" fill="#6c685f" font-size="10" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">a query</text>
          <text x="364" y="155" fill="#514d45" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">60%</text>

          <rect x="212" y="203" width="168" height="44" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="228" y="222" fill="#514d45" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Rule</text>
          <text x="228" y="238" fill="#6c685f" font-size="10" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">a query</text>
          <text x="364" y="230" fill="#514d45" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">20%</text>

          <!-- Rules -> Scorecard (converge) -->
          <line x1="382" y1="75" x2="556" y2="150" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#scArrow)"/>
          <line x1="382" y1="150" x2="556" y2="150" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#scArrow)"/>
          <line x1="382" y1="225" x2="556" y2="150" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#scArrow)"/>

          <!-- Scorecard node -->
          <rect x="560" y="110" width="180" height="80" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#0041d9" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="578" y="140" fill="#0041d9" font-size="14" font-weight="700">Scorecard</text>
          <text x="578" y="158" fill="#6c685f" font-size="10" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">avg of rules</text>
          <text x="722" y="182" fill="#0041d9" font-size="20" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">40%</text>

          <!-- Annotations -->
          <text x="24" y="273" fill="#6c685f" font-size="11" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">&#9679; pass&#160;&#160;&#9675; fail</text>
          <text x="212" y="273" fill="#6c685f" font-size="11" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">rule = passed &#247; checked</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        The scoring model: entities roll up into a rule score, rules average into a scorecard score
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <Callout label="The catch">
      It's a flexible, composable model: the same service can be measured
      against different criteria in different scorecards. Powerful for
      customers, and the reason leveling wasn't a simple add-on.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="02" title="A more flexible model, a harder problem">
    <Prose>
      <h3>How competitors do it</h3>
      <p>
        Before designing anything, I benchmarked Cortex, Rely.io, and Port. All
        three gamify Scorecards the same way: a service{" "}
        <strong>attaches directly to a scorecard</strong> and climbs named
        levels (Bronze → Silver → Gold) as it passes more of that scorecard's
        checks. The level lives on the entity, scoped to one scorecard.
      </p>
      <h3>Why that doesn't work for New Relic</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Entities aren't scored by a scorecard directly</strong> —
          they're scored by its rules.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A service isn't simply "in" a scorecard</strong> the way it
          is in Cortex; it sits in the intersection of that scorecard's rules.
        </li>
        <li>
          That's deliberate: rule-level scoring is what lets customers compose
          rules freely and reuse entities across scorecards. But it also means
          {" "}<strong>there's no single "level" baked into a scorecard for an
          entity to climb</strong> — the flexibility that makes the model
          powerful is exactly what made leveling hard.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
    <figure class="mt-8 mb-14">
      <div class="grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <!-- Competitor model -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Competitors</p>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="mt-3 w-full" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif">
            <!-- Scorecard -->
            <rect x="120" y="14" width="120" height="38" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#514d45">Scorecard</text>
            <!-- connectors -->
            <line x1="180" y1="52" x2="65" y2="130" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="52" x2="180" y2="130" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="52" x2="295" y2="130" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- entities carry a level -->
            <rect x="22" y="130" width="86" height="44" rx="8" fill="#f7f5f0" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="42" cy="152" r="5" fill="#b06b3a"/>
            <text x="54" y="156" font-size="12" fill="#514d45">Entity</text>
            <text x="65" y="169" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">Bronze</text>
            <rect x="137" y="130" width="86" height="44" rx="8" fill="#f7f5f0" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="157" cy="152" r="5" fill="#9aa0a6"/>
            <text x="169" y="156" font-size="12" fill="#514d45">Entity</text>
            <text x="180" y="169" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">Silver</text>
            <rect x="252" y="130" width="86" height="44" rx="8" fill="#f7f5f0" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="272" cy="152" r="5" fill="#c9a227"/>
            <text x="284" y="156" font-size="12" fill="#514d45">Entity</text>
            <text x="295" y="169" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">Gold</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The entity attaches directly to a scorecard, so its level (Bronze,
            Silver, or Gold) is just an attribute there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- New Relic model -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-[#0041d9]">New Relic</p>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="mt-3 w-full" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif">
            <!-- Scorecard -->
            <rect x="120" y="10" width="120" height="36" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#0041d9" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="33" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0041d9">Scorecard</text>
            <!-- scorecard -> rules -->
            <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="65" y2="86" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="180" y2="86" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="295" y2="86" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- rules -->
            <rect x="30" y="86" width="70" height="30" rx="7" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="65" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#514d45">Rule</text>
            <rect x="145" y="86" width="70" height="30" rx="7" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#514d45">Rule</text>
            <rect x="260" y="86" width="70" height="30" rx="7" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="295" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#514d45">Rule</text>
            <!-- rules -> entities -->
            <line x1="65" y1="116" x2="65" y2="140" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="116" x2="180" y2="140" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="295" y1="116" x2="295" y2="140" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- entity dots per rule -->
            <circle cx="50" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="65" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="80" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="165" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="180" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="195" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="280" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="295" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="310" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <text x="180" y="182" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">entities&#160;(&#9679;&#160;pass&#160;&#160;&#9675;&#160;fail)</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Entities are scored through rules, so there is no single entity level
            to attach a badge to.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        Why leveling is straightforward for competitors, but not for New Relic
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <Callout label="The real question">
      New Relic's model is multi-dimensional: entity → rule → scorecard, then grouped by
      team or not at all. How do we surface leveling gracefully across every one
      of those dimensions, without overwhelming the interface?
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="03" title="How a team levels up">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        If entities are scored through rules, the rule is the only place a
        level can live. So working with the PM, EM, and the team's architect,
        we attached leveling to the <strong>rule</strong>, not the scorecard:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Each rule in a scorecard is assigned to a <strong>maturity level</strong>:
          Below basic, Basic, Intermediate, or Advanced.
        </li>
        <li>
          An entity's maturity level is the <strong>highest level whose rules it's
          passing</strong>, so progress is something a team can actually climb.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Weights</strong>, set up through the API, let a compliance
          manager mark which rules matter most, so the score and the level reflect real priorities rather than
          treating every rule as equal.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>

    <Callout label="A trade-off I made on scope">
      For weights, I made a conscious call to only support setup through the
      API, not a UI. A setup screen would have added real complexity and
      cognitive load for a feature compliance managers don't use regularly.
      The trade-off: I cut that UI convenience in return for shipping levels
      and the leaderboard without piling on even more new UIs.
    </Callout>

    <Prose class="mt-6">
      <p>
        I designed the setup flow: an author drags each rule into the level it
        belongs to.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/25 p-6">
      <Figure
        src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leveling-setup.mp4"
        caption="Setup: assigning each rule to a maturity level (Below basic → Advanced)"
        class="mx-auto max-w-2xl my-0"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-start">
      <Figure
        src="/images/scorecard-gamification/scorecard-detail.jpg"
        caption="Scorecard view: rules grouped by the maturity level they define"
        class="my-0"
      />
      <Figure
        src="/images/scorecard-gamification/entity-maturity.png"
        caption="Entity view: maturity distribution and per-entity progress"
        class="my-0"
      />
    </div>

    <Callout>
      Levels don't live on the scorecard; they live on its rules. That one move let
      leveling ride on top of New Relic's flexible model instead of requiring us to
      rebuild it.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="04" title="Designing how teams compare (iterations and testing)">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Levels and weights answered <em>how a team makes progress</em>. The
        leaderboard asked a different question: when we crown a team, what exactly
        are we ranking? It's the piece that changed the most between my early
        explorations and what shipped.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of my early directions leaned into richness, each trying a different
        way to pack in badges, levels, and per-rule detail:
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-4">
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">A</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/4.png"
            alt="Leaderboard with a podium and per-team badge counts"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">B</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/3.png"
            alt="Leaderboard with named tier badges and rank numbers"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">C</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/2.png"
            alt="Leaderboard encoding level through filled and faded badges, with per-rule detail"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">D</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/1.png"
            alt="Leaderboard breaking every scorecard into per-level bar charts"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">A. Medals + badge counts</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          A podium plus a row of badge counts per level. Showed breadth of
          achievement, but counting badges rewarded teams that simply owned more
          scorecards, and the row was busy to read.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">B. Named tier badges</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Memorable identities like “DevOps guru” or “The innovator.” Characterful,
          but the label needed decoding, and pairing it with rank numbers and
          scores was hard to correlate.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">C. Badge transparency</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Level encoded with filled vs. faded badges, expandable to per-rule
          detail. Precise, but the transparency states were subtle and the detail
          dense.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">D. Per-level bar charts</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Every scorecard broken into Basic / Bronze / Silver / Gold bars. The most
          detailed design, and the hardest to scan; it makes it hard to know who's
          ahead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-14">
      <p>
        A quick round of internal testing made the pattern clear. Even engineering
        managers, the people closest to this data, struggled to connect the badges,
        icons, and numbers to what they actually meant.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <Callout label="The trade-off">
      The final leaderboard gives up inline per-scorecard detail to gain a
      comparison that reads in a second. Naming that trade-off is what let the team
      land on it with confidence, and know exactly what they'd trade to get the
      detail back later.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="05" title="What shipped">
    <Prose>
      <p>The design shipped as a connected set of capabilities:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maturity levels</strong> — rules assigned to Below basic →
          Advanced, so teams have a level to climb rather than a flat percentage.</li>
        <li><strong>Configurable weights</strong> — compliance managers prioritize
          the rules that carry the most risk, so the score reflects what actually
          matters.</li>
        <li><strong>Team leaderboard</strong> — teams ranked by one accumulated
          score to foster healthy competition and drive operational excellence.</li>
        <li><strong>Entity progression &amp; reporting</strong> — maturity
          distribution, per-entity profiles, and rule performance over time show
          where each service stands and where to invest next.</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/25 p-6">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
        <p class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-[#0041d9]">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-lg leading-none">✦</span> Shipped
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leaderboard-demo.mp4"
          caption="From the scorecards overview to the team leaderboard, live in production"
          class="my-0"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-4 gap-4">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">Group by team</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Switching Group by from None to Team is what turns individual
          scorecards into a team-level leaderboard.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">Podium spotlight</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The top three teams get a medal, a team score, and how many entities
          they cover, so recognition is instant.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">Score vs. growth ranking</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          A toggle separates who's highest right now from who's improved the
