        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">7 days / 1 month / 3 months</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Switching the time window shows whether a team's climb is a blip or
          a real trend.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="06" title="Outcomes and reflection">
    <figure class="mx-auto mb-12 max-w-3xl text-center">
      <blockquote
        class="font-display text-[24px] font-medium italic leading-snug tracking-tight text-ink"
      >
        “The distinctions between basic, intermediate, and mature… that's my
        favorite thing. It helps teams not try to do everything all at once, in
        any order. There's a sequence to this, and a measured way to do it.”
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="mt-5 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        Engineering leader, customer validation session
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <Callout label="What moved">
      Scorecards usage rose 12% after launch, and advanced Compute Consumption
      Units (aCCU), the metric the suite is priced on, rose 8%. Gamification
      moved the number that actually mattered to the business, not just an
      engagement stat.
    </Callout>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        If I could redo one part of this, I'd have the data-model conversation
        with Engineering <strong>before</strong> sketching anything, not after
        a scrapped first pass. Benchmarking competitors was useful, but
        building toward their pattern before checking it against our own model
        cost real time.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
